\paragraph{Gradient hierarchy.}
\looseness=-1 The gradient-condition analysis (Table~\ref{tab:three_way_emb}) reveals that CIVO is not attributable to any single gradient pathway. Reward-only detachment, which blocks reward-head gradients while preserving observation and KL pathways, still produces CIVO onset (NVE $4.45\times$), demonstrating that the reward head is not the sole transmission channel. Full-SGS, restricting gradient flow to the KL pathway alone, partially mitigates severity in one seed (NVE $1.66\times$) but fails to prevent collapse in the other. Only completely freezing or removing the embedding (0/10 seeds, NVE ${\leq}1.88\times$) eliminates CIVO, confirming that gradient-driven co-adaptation between the embedding and world model---rather than any specific loss component---is the root cause.

\paragraph{Cross-environment scope boundary.}
\looseness=-1 Single-joint Cartpole Swingup produces 0/3 CIVO under the identical trigger configuration (trained $e_{\dt}$, subsample ON, $H{=}8$), despite the same hyperparameters triggering CIVO in all 10 tested multi-joint locomotion seeds. Three ManiSkill manipulation tasks exhibit value-ratio transients of comparable magnitude ($4$--$11\times$) in both trained and frozen controls (Table~\ref{tab:cross_domain}), indicating environment-specific dynamics rather than embedding-driven pathology. Together with the locomotion results, these findings establish that CIVO requires both a trained temporal embedding \emph{and} multi-joint dynamics where temporal abstraction amplifies reward fidelity gaps across coordinated joints.

